{
  "$schema": "https://vega.github.io/schema/vega-lite/v6.json",
  "description": "Three geographic small multiples showing new and previously dedicated National Surfing Reserves over time.",
  "usermeta": {
    "title": "How Did Surfing Reserve Recognition Spread Across Australia?",
    "analysis": "The first phase of National Surfing Reserve recognition was exclusively NSW-based: all eight sites dedicated between 2006 and 2009 were in that state. The 2010–2013 phase marked a decisive geographic expansion, adding eight more reserves and bringing Western Australia, Queensland, South Australia and Victoria into the register. Margaret River's 2010 dedication was the first in WA, extending the initiative from Australia's east coast to its west. By 2013, 16 sites had been recognised across five states. The final phase added Noosa (2015), Bondi (2017) and Norah Head (2022), consolidating the eastern coastal network rather than extending into new states. The three equal-scale maps reveal a transition from a NSW-centred initiative to a national coastal heritage program, driven by communities recognising and protecting their established surfing places.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "label": "National Surfing Reserves — Official Register",
        "url": "https://surfingreserves.org.au/national-surfing-reserves/"
      },
      {
        "label": "Margaret River — First WA Dedication",
        "url": "https://surfingreserves.org.au/margaret-river/"
      }
    ]
  },
  "background": "#ffffff",
  "spacing": 14,
